  "result": "`python3 -m shop quote a:12.5:2` now prints `27.06`, and all 10 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. For `\"12.5\"`, `\"5\".rjust(2, \"0\")` gives `\"05\"`, so the price became 1205 cents instead of 1250. The quote came out as 26.09, because 2 \u00d7 12.05 plus tax is 26.09. Prices with two decimals or none were unaffected. That is why the existing tests never caught it.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `rjust` to `ljust`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`.\n\nI also added `tests/test_money.py`, which covers one decimal, two decimals, none, and too many decimals. The one-decimal test fails on the old code.\n\n`to_cents` doesn't handle negative amounts correctly: `\"-0.50\"` comes out as +50 cents. Nothing in the CLI passes a negative price, so I left it alone.",
